65625, 0.015960693359375, 0.0156707763671875, -0.0038547515869140625, 0.0044403076171875, -0.0152740478515625, -0.00788116455078125, -0.00592803955078125, -0.0036869049072265625, 0.0011272430419921875, -0.016510009765625, 0.01387786865234375, -0.005733489990234375, -0.00826263427734375, 0.00949859619140625, -0.0090789794921875, 0.00563812255859375, 0.033538818359375, -0.036834716796875, -0.0035839080810546875, -0.01311492919921875, -0.00756072998046875, -0.01012420654296875, 0.004352569580078125, -0.00884246826171875, 0.00714874267578125, 0.0251922607421875, -0.047393798828125, -0.011444091796875, -0.00846099853515625, -0.01190948486328125, 0.0213775634765625, -0.0160064697265625, 0.02166748046875, -0.037689208984375, 0.013702392578125, -0.0196990966796875, 0.0099029541015625, -0.0017709732055664062, -0.0121002197265625, -0.0032138824462890625, -0.00926971435546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Main Results**\n\nThis short work presents a novel extension of the permanental process model to include fixed effects, enabling both structured covariate information and flexible nonparametric modeling within a unified framework. A *permanental process* is a class of Cox processes where the intensity function is modeled via the exponential of a Gaussian process, and the likelihood involves the permanent of a kernel matrix (see [McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006] for background). Our main mathematical result is the following: *In the diffuse prior limit for the fixed effects coefficients, the intensity function of the permanental process admits a unique decomposition into a fixed effects term and a nonparametric term in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS), and the regularization penalty on the nonparametric component is given by the squared RKHS norm.* This result clarifies how structured and unstructured effects can be separated in estimation.\n\n**Proposition 1.** *Let \\( \\lambda(x) \\) denote the intensity function of the permanental process, modeled as \\( \\log \\lambda(x) = \\beta^T z(x) + f(x) \\), where \\( z(x) \\) are known covariates, \\( \\beta \\) are fixed effects coefficients, and \\( f \\) lies in an RKHS \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\) with kernel \\( k \\). Assume: (i) the likelihood is differentiable and convex in \\( f \\) and \\( \\beta \\); (ii) the data locations \\( x_i \\) are fixed and observed; (iii) the kernel \\( k \\) is positive definite; and (iv) the prior on \\( \\beta \\) is Gaussian with covariance \\( \\tau^2 I \\) and \\( \\tau^2 \\to \\infty \\) (the diffuse prior limit). Then the penalized likelihood estimator for \\( f \\) and \\( \\beta \\) is equivalent to minimizing the negative log-likelihood plus \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\), with no penalty on \\( \\beta \\).*\n\nA *penalized likelihood estimator* here refers to the minimizer of the sum of the negative log-likelihood and a regularization term, typically used to control model complexity.\n\n**2. The Diffuse Prior Limit**\n\nThe \"diffuse prior limit\" refers to the case where the prior variance \\( \\tau^2 \\) on the fixed effects coefficients \\( \\beta \\) tends to infinity, making the prior non-informative. Statistically, this means that the posterior for \\( \\beta \\) is determined solely by the likelihood, with no regularization or shrinkage imposed by the prior. In this limit, the estimation of \\( \\beta \\) is based entirely on the observed data and the likelihood function, while the regularization penalty applies only to the nonparametric component \\( f \\). This separation ensures that fixed effects are estimated without bias from prior assumptions, but it may lead to overfitting if the number of covariates is large relative to the data.\n\n**3. Representer Theorem and Model Structure**\n\nThe representer theorem states that the solution to regularized empirical risk minimization problems in an RKHS can be written as a finite linear combination of kernel functions evaluated at the observed data points. In our context, this means that the optimal \\( f \\) minimizing the penalized likelihood (with penalty \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\)) can be expressed as\n\\[\nf^*(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i k(x, x_i),\n\\]\nwhere \\( x_i \\) are the observed data locations and \\( \\alpha_i \\) are coefficients determined by the data and the fixed effects. This result holds under the assumptions that the kernel \\( k \\) is positive definite, the likelihood is differentiable and convex in \\( f \\), and the data locations are fixed. If these conditions are violated (e.g., non-convex likelihood, non-positive-definite kernel), the representer theorem may not apply, and the solution may not admit a finite representation.\n\n**4. RKHS Penalty and Kernel Choice**\n\nThe squared RKHS norm \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\) serves as the regularization penalty for the nonparametric component. For example, if \\( k \\) is the squared exponential kernel, \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\) penalizes roughness in \\( f \\), favoring smooth functions. The choice of kernel directly impacts the flexibility and smoothness of the nonparametric term; for instance, a Mat\u00e9rn kernel allows for varying degrees of smoothness, which can be tailored to the application domain. In practice, kernel selection can be guided by prior knowledge, cross-validation, or marginal likelihood maximization (see [Rasmussen & Williams, 2006]). However, kernel misspecification can lead to poor fit or misleading inference, so sensitivity analysis or model comparison is recommended.\n\n**5. Practical Implications and Example**\n\nThis decomposition has important implications for interpretation and modeling. By isolating the fixed effects term, practitioners can directly assess the impact of known covariates, while the RKHS component captures complex, unknown structure in the data. For example, in ecological modeling, fixed effects might represent known environmental variables (e.g., temperature, elevation), while the RKHS term models spatial variation.\n\nTo illustrate, we simulated a spatial point process on the unit square with intensity \\( \\lambda(x) = \\exp(\\beta z(x) + f_0(x)) \\), where \\( z(x) \\) is a standardized covariate (e.g., elevation), \\( \\beta = 1 \\), and \\( f_0(x) \\) is a smooth spatial effect drawn from a Gaussian process with squared exponential kernel. We generated 200 points and fit the extended permanental process model. The estimated \\( \\beta \\) closely matched the true value, and the recovered \\( f \\) accurately captured the underlying spatial pattern, demonstrating the model's ability to disentangle fixed and nonparametric effects (see Supplementary Figure 1 for details).\n\n**6. Applications, Limitations, and Extensions**\n\nOur extension enables more interpretable and flexible permanental process models, broadening their applicability to scientific domains where both fixed and nonparametric effects are relevant. For instance, in spatial statistics, one can model disease incidence as a function of demographic covariates (fixed effects) and spatial clustering (RKHS term). In survival analysis, baseline covariates can be separated from time-varying risk captured nonparametrically. However, challenges may arise with high-dimensional covariates, non-Gaussian likelihoods, or non-stationary kernels, requiring adaptation of the estimation procedure or additional regularization. Extensions to other point process models or to non-Euclidean domains are possible but may require careful consideration of kernel properties and computational tractability.\n\n**7. Related Work**\n\nPrevious work on permanental processes has focused primarily on nonparametric modeling of intensity functions (see [McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006]; [Shirai & Takahashi, 2003]). The incorporation of fixed effects into Cox and log-Gaussian Cox processes is well-studied (e.g., [M\u00f8ller et al., 1998]), but explicit treatment in the permanental process context has been limited. Our contribution clarifies the mathematical structure and estimation in this setting, connecting to broader literature on semiparametric models and RKHS regularization (see [Wahba, 1990]).\n\n**8. Conclusion**\n\nIn summary, we provide a precise mathematical framework for incorporating fixed effects into permanental process models via the diffuse prior limit, clarify the role of the representer theorem and RKHS penalty, and illustrate the practical benefits with concrete examples. This extension enhances interpretability and flexibility, making permanental process models more broadly applicable in domains requiring both structured and nonparametric modeling.\n\n**References**\n\n- McCullagh, P., & M\u00f8ller, J. (2006). The permanental process. *Advances in Applied Probability*, 38(4), 873\u2013888.\n- Shirai, T., & Takahashi, Y. (2003). Random point fields associated with certain Fredholm determinants I: Fermion, Poisson and boson point processes. *Journal of Functional Analysis*, 205(2), 414\u2013463.\n- M\u00f8ller, J., Syversveen, A. R., & Waagepetersen, R. P. (1998). Log Gaussian Cox processes. *Scandinavian Journal of Statistics*, 25(3), 451\u2013482.\n- Rasmussen, C. E., & Williams, C. K. I. (2006). *Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning*. MIT Press.\n- Wahba, G. (1990). *Spline Models for Observational Data*. SIAM."}}